# Keep line endings deterministic across checkouts.
#
# The machine .toml files are embedded byte-for-byte into the binary by
# cmake/embed_machines.cmake (as a raw unsigned-char array, HEX-read). A Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true would rewrite them CRLF and change the embedded
# bytes -- so pin all text to LF, and mark the binary media so git never touches it.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.toml text eol=lf
*.exp  text eol=lf
*.cmake text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf

# .bat files are Windows-only and only ever run by cmd.exe. Store them CRLF so a
# checkout matches the platform they belong to -- the native convention there, and the
# one line ending cmd.exe is guaranteed to parse without surprises around labels/goto.
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# CP/M source and listing artifacts (.ASM, .PRN, .HEX, .INC, .Z80, .LST) originated on a CP/M system
# and are meant to be transferred verbatim onto a CP/M disk -- pasted with PIP x=CON:, run
# through the assembler's LOAD, or carried in a bootable image -- where CP/M expects CRLF
# line terminators (and, for text, a trailing ^Z). The global `eol=lf` rule above would
# strip them to LF on checkout, which broke `PIP R.ASM=CON:`: the pasted source arrived as
# one line with no terminators, so ASM emitted nothing (issue #118). Mark them binary so
# git preserves the exact CRLF bytes on every platform and never normalises them. (.HEX is
# already binary in the media section below.)
*.ASM binary
*.asm binary
*.PRN binary
*.prn binary
*.INC binary
*.inc binary
*.Z80 binary
*.z80 binary
*.LST binary
*.lst binary

# The CP/M symbol-file fixtures carry two more format-under-test extensions
# (tests/programs/symbols/README.md): their CRLF and trailing ^Z padding are part of the
# format, so keep these binary too. (.ASM/.PRN there are covered by the rule above.)
tests/programs/symbols/*.SYM binary
tests/programs/symbols/*.MAC binary

# The MBL boot ROM's source and listing are deramp originals, CRLF like every other
# period artifact here (roms/MBL/). Keep them byte-for-byte so the checkout matches the
# dump on every platform. (.HEX is binary globally below.) These are scoped to roms/MBL
# on purpose: the repo-wide `*.ASM`/`*.PRN binary` rule is a separate change.
roms/MBL/*.PRN binary
roms/MBL/*.ASM binary

# Period media and disk images are binary -- never munge.
*.DSK binary
*.dsk binary
*.bin binary
*.BIN binary
*.hex binary
*.HEX binary
# Motorola S-records -- the Altair 680b's ROM format (roms/MON680, roms/SWIMON). A period
# ROM image like .HEX: fetched byte-for-byte from deramp, CRLF and all. The decoded image
# is line-ending agnostic, but the artifact is committed verbatim so the checkout matches
# the dump on every platform. Keep binary so the global eol=lf never rewrites it.
*.s19 binary
*.S19 binary
*.srec binary
*.SREC binary
*.keys binary
*.tap binary
*.TAP binary
*.wav binary
*.WAV binary
*.pdf binary
*.PDF binary

# The TREK80 ENTER script is a period artifact fetched byte-for-byte from the archive
# (docs/sources.md), CRLF line endings and all, and examples/sol/make-trek80-tape.sh
# derives the shipped tape from it. Normalising it would change the source of a
# reproducible build.
examples/sol/TREK80.ENT binary
